# .mutation-allowlist - surviving (LIVED) mutants accepted as already known.
#
# SCOPE: this allowlist covers ONLY the packages inside the mutation gate scope
# (see MUTATE_EXCLUDE in the Makefile): internal/cli, internal/db, internal/model,
# internal/config and internal/tui. Mutations outside that scope are not run at
# all, so they can never appear here. cmd/ is still excluded: it is the entry point
# with no logic of its own.
#
# Format:  <MUTATOR> <file>:<line>
#
# Add an entry ONLY when the mutant is provably equivalent (no test can ever kill
# it, because the mutation does not change observable behaviour) or the gap is a
# conscious, accepted risk. Adding a real test gap here hides a real risk, and
# mutation testing exists precisely to surface those.
#
# Entries are keyed by LINE NUMBER, so inserting code above an entry shifts it
# and will surface a new red on purpose: re-verify the gap and update this file,
# do not regenerate it wholesale (that would silently re-hide real gaps).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# internal/tui
#
# The TUI went into the gate at 47 survivors out of 937, from 472 when the work
# started. Every entry below was hand-verified by applying the mutation and
# running the suite. They fall into six shapes, and knowing the shape is what
# tells you whether the entry can ever go away.
#
# 1. "THE BOUNDARY COINCIDES". Both branches produce the same value at the
#    boundary, so no input separates them: slicing a slice at exactly its own
#    length, padding with zero spaces, clipping a string at exactly its width.
#
# 2. "THE CONTAINER CLIPS". The content is truncated to a bound and then put in a
#    fixed-width box, and the box clips anything that does not fit. Measured: with
#    the detail modal's comment bound moved two columns wider, the render came out
#    byte-identical -- same line count, same widths. A bound inside the container
#    is therefore invisible, which is the opposite of what a test of the bound
#    itself would assume.
#
# 3. "THE STATE NEVER HAPPENS". The two sides differ only on a value the program
#    cannot produce: an index inside a guard that already checks it, a comparison
#    on a count that is a map of counters and so never negative, a key Bubbletea
#    does not deliver as a single character.
#
# 4. "TWO BRANCHES, SAME RESULT". The mutation picks a different way of reaching
#    the value that is already there: a capacity hint, a multiply by one, an
#    early return whose body was a no-op anyway.
#
# 5. "UNIQUE KEYS". A sort comparator made non-strict (`<` into `<=`). The keys
#    come from a map, so two of them are never equal and the two forms order
#    identically.
#
# 6. ACCEPTED RISK. Two of them. Marked as such below, because they are gaps and
#    not equivalences, and pretending otherwise is how real risks get hidden.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE BOUNDARY COINCIDES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `if len(descLines) > descBudget` -> `>=`. At exactly the budget, descLines[:budget]
# is the same slice. Note that `detailHeightBudget` guarantees at least two lines
# are always left for the description, so a zero descBudget is not reachable
# either.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/detail.go:67

# `if len(commentLines) > commentBudget` -> `>=`. Same slice identity, same reason
# the comment budget can never be zero.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/detail.go:73

# `if totalLines < h` -> `<=`. At equal height the padding is zero newlines, which
# is what the branch would have produced anyway.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/detail.go:111

# `if visible <= 0 || end-start <= visible` -> `<`. Both halves are identities at
# their boundary: a window of exactly `visible` is the whole page, and a window of
# zero renders as [pageStart, pageEnd) through the same arithmetic.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/layout.go:273
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/layout.go:273
INVERT_NEGATIVES internal/tui/layout.go:273

# `if total <= 0 || size <= 0` -> `<`. Only the size half matters: with size == 0
# the general path returns [start, start) with start clamped to the cursor, which
# is not (0, 0) in general. The total half is the redundant one -- at total == 0
# the `size = min(size, total)` below already reduces it to the same (0, 0).
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/layout.go:662

# `if len(items) > max` / `if len(out) > max` -> `>=`. Truncating a slice to
# exactly its own length returns the same slice. The callers pass max > 0, and
# max == 0 takes the other branch.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/fuzzy.go:41
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/fuzzy.go:63

# `if len(wrapped) > maxLines` -> `>=`. Same slice identity: wrapping to exactly
# maxLines lines and slicing to maxLines is the same thing.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/previewbar.go:81

# `if len(suggs) > tagMaxSuggestions` -> `>=`. suggs[:tagMaxSuggestions] at exactly
# the cap is suggs.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/tag_modal.go:125

# `if len(opts) <= 1` -> `<`. With a single option the cycle lands back on index
# zero, which is where it started, and there is always at least one option
# because "all" is an aggregator that survives any search. Both forms set the
# project filter to "all".
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/filter_modal.go:177

# `if free <= 0` -> `<`. At exactly zero the distribution that follows adds zero
# to every column and its remainder loop runs zero times. Removing the guard is
# not an option: a negative free would then subtract from the minimums.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/kanban.go:172

# `if titleWidth > innerWidth` -> `>=`. ansi.Truncate at exactly the width returns
# the string unchanged.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/bordered/bordered.go:110

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. THE CONTAINER CLIPS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `truncateLines(line, width-2)` with the bound moved by +/- 2. The comment lines
# go into a box of fixed width, and that box clips rather than wraps: measured,
# moving the bound two columns wider produced a byte-identical render -- same line
# count, same line widths.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/detail.go:133
INVERT_NEGATIVES internal/tui/detail.go:133

# `truncateLines(card, width-4)`, same reason: the card goes inside the column's
# box and the column box clips it.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/kanban.go:212
INVERT_NEGATIVES internal/tui/kanban.go:212

# `renderFilterHeader(w-2)`. The bar's natural width does not depend on this
# argument -- the separator and the bar are both padded to the widest of the
# joined block -- so the only thing the bound controls is the number of dashes,
# which the surrounding frame does not expose.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/kanban.go:130
INVERT_NEGATIVES internal/tui/kanban.go:130

# `minWidths[i] = lipgloss.Width(headers[i]) + 2` -> `+ 1`. The minimums of every
# column move together, and so does the shared slack: with n columns, free/n
# shrinks by exactly one while each minimum grows by exactly one, so every width
# comes out the same. This is an identity, not an approximation.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/kanban.go:112

# `if i < len(cols)-1` -> `i <= len(cols)-1` / `len(cols)`. The margin on the last
# column falls outside the frame, which is clipped to the view's width.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/kanban.go:121
INVERT_NEGATIVES internal/tui/kanban.go:121
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/kanban.go:121

# `ansi.Truncate(wrapped[len-1], limit-1, "")`. Defensive: ansi.Wrap has already
# returned lines of at most the limit, so the Truncate removes nothing and its
# exact bound does not matter. What does the work here is the ellipsis appended
# after it.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/previewbar.go:87
INVERT_NEGATIVES internal/tui/previewbar.go:87

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. THE STATE NEVER HAPPENS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `if len(key) == 1 && key[0] >= 32` -> `>= 33`. Bubbletea never delivers a
# one-character key for the space: it reports "space", five characters, which the
# `len == 1` test discards. So the lower bound has nothing below it to exclude.
# This is also why a space cannot be typed into the tag input -- a real bug, with
# a test that pins the current behaviour and says so.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/tag_modal.go:88

# The same bound in the filter modal, where it is deliberate: the fields are
# comma-separated lists and a space would break them.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/filter_modal.go:373

# `if len(t.Tags) > 0` -> `>= 0`. With no tags the branch appends two spaces to a
# line the column pads to its width anyway, so the extra spaces are not visible.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/kanban.go:200

# `for i := m.ganttCursor + dir; i >= 0 && ...` -> `i > 0`. Row zero of the Gantt
# is always an assignee header: ganttRows emits the header before that person's
# tasks, and only for people who have entries. So the row the mutant skips can
# never be a task row, and skipping it changes nothing.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/gantt.go:134

# `m.ganttCursor += 1 + i` -> `+ 0` / `- i`. i is the offset of the first task row
# after the cursor, and the row after a header is always a task row, so i is
# always 0 and the addition is always 1.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/gantt.go:98

# `make([]rune, labelW+1+dayCols)` -> `labelW*1+dayCols`. Multiplying by one.
# (The additive variants of this line are killable and are covered: the ruler's
# width is asserted, and the Monday label's column is pinned.)
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/tui/gantt.go:254

# `err == nil && e >= 0` -> `e > 0`. The line assigns `estimate = e` where
# estimate already holds 0, so the only input that separates the two comparisons
# is e == 0, and writing a zero over a zero changes nothing. A negative is
# rejected by both.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/editor.go:139

# `m.toastSeq++` -> `--`. The counter's contract is that each toast gets a number
# the stale tick does not carry, not that the numbers are consecutive. The test
# fires two toasts and checks the first one's tick does not clear the second.
INCREMENT_DECREMENT internal/tui/toast.go:21

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. TWO BRANCHES, SAME RESULT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `if width < 2` -> `< 1`. With the floor, a width of 1 becomes 2 and the inner
# width is 0; without it, 1 - 1 - 1 is -1 and the floor below brings it to 0 as
# well. The two are the same, which is what makes the floor below redundant too.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/bordered/bordered.go:29

# `if innerWidth < 0` -> `< -1`. Only reachable with width == 1, which the floor
# above turns into 2. It stays because strings.Repeat panics on a negative count.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/bordered/bordered.go:61

# `if padding < 0 { padding = 0 }` -> `<= 0`. wrapLine never returns a chunk wider
# than the limit it was given, so the padding is never negative. Defensive, for
# the same reason the other two are.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/bordered/bordered.go:153

# `if k := strings.Index(s, csiPrefix); k >= 0` -> `> 0`. k can never be 0 here:
# a string starting with the CSI prefix was already handled by the branch above,
# so this search only runs on a string that does not start with it.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/bordered/bordered.go:214

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. UNIQUE KEYS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `result[i].Name < result[j].Name` -> `<=`. The roster is built from a map keyed
# by name, so two entries never compare equal and a strict and a non-strict
# comparator order them identically.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/layout.go:478

# The same, for the fuzzy ranking: `score >` and `item <` made non-strict. Equal
# scores fall through to the name comparison, and the order among equals is the
# input order either way -- which TestFuzzyFilterTieBreaksAlphabetically and
# TestFuzzyFilterKeepsInputOrderOnFullTie both pin.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/fuzzy.go:59
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/tui/fuzzy.go:61

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. ACCEPTED RISK -- gaps, not equivalences
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `if _, err := tmpFile.WriteString(...); err != nil` -> `err == nil`, and the
# same for the Close right below. Provoking these needs a full disk or a double
# close, and the function takes no seam to inject through: it calls os.CreateTemp,
# WriteString and Close directly. Both branches do the same thing as the
# CreateTemp branch above them, which IS covered -- the test points TMPDIR at a
# directory that does not exist and gets the error with the task id.
#
# The equivalent is to take an *os.File, or the three operations, as parameters.
# That is a real change to production code for no behaviour difference, so it is
# left as a gap and named here instead of being hidden.
CONDITIONALS_NEGATION internal/tui/editor.go:37
CONDITIONALS_NEGATION internal/tui/editor.go:44

